  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Thursday)\nIssued at 1224 PM CST FRI NOV 20 2026\n\nThrough the weekend, most of our weather will be driven by the\norganized disturbance off the east coast of Florida. As this\ndisturbance moves north (and eventually inland), a few rouge\nshowers may work into our far SE zones, with the bulk of the\nrainfall remaining to our east. Winds during this time will also\nshift to the NE, allowing cooler, drier air to filter down the\nAppalachian Mountains, slightly lowering humidity values in these\nareas.\n\nDuring this same timeframe to our west, a trough and cold front\nwill begin to move through the Plains. Come Monday, this cold\nfront will become elongated and stall somewhere just outside of\nthe state. This will allow for temperatures to drop slightly,\nespecially in locations closest to the front, and for slightly\nincreased rain chances in our NW areas Sunday night. While\nconfidence in the overall placement of this front, and how far\neast the rain spreads is low, we should at least have some\neffects through Tuesday. By Wednesday, confidence increases that\nthis stalled front will finally work through the region, with\ntemperatures taking another swing in a much cooler direction.\nExpect these cooler conditions for your Thanksgiving, with little\nto no rainfall currently in the forecast for Thursday.\n\n/44/",
